WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Vsauce! Kevin here. We’re in an era where&nbsp;
crime scene investigators, lab techs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.160
and detectives seem almost superhuman&nbsp;
— some of the best shows on TV blend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.340
forensics and data to analyze criminal&nbsp;
behavior to a remarkable degree. You’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.920
think everyone is Sherlock Holmes. And&nbsp;
it is true that our use of science and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.520
math to solve crimes — and to understand the&nbsp;
people who commit them — is advancing rapidly.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.720
But Jennifer Thompson’s eyewitness&nbsp;
identification of her attacker was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.720
flat-out wrong — and a broken police lineup&nbsp;
methodology encouraged an error that put Ronald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.200
Cotton in prison for a decade. Both a judge&nbsp;
and jury misunderstanding simple probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.480
convicted Janet Collins for an assault&nbsp;
and robbery she had nothing to do with.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:58.440
The truth is that as we advance, we run&nbsp;
into serious problems, new limitations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.960
and unintended consequences. I’ve&nbsp;
spent a year studying stories that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.400
reveal the delicate balance of&nbsp;
scientific progress and justice.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.920
These seven videos have been compiled&nbsp;
into one longform viewing session for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.180
your convenience and I’ll be back at&nbsp;
the end to discuss what we’ve learned.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.460
First, imagine the FBI arresting you for&nbsp;
an act of terrorism that happened over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.260
5,000 miles away -- and insisting&nbsp;
that international authorities have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:34.320
somehow forensically tied you to a bomb on&nbsp;
another continent. You went to bed innocent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.240
But because of faulty fingerprint&nbsp;
analysis, you woke up a terrorist.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.880
Vsauce, Kevin here. Imagine the horror of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.500
arrested in connection to a crime&nbsp;
the entire world is reading about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.980
on the basis of irrefutable fingerprint&nbsp;
evidence, that you KNOW you didn’t commit.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.080
How do you defend yourself against a&nbsp;
century of science and math that just…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.840
aren’t wrong? And how can you possibly&nbsp;
win when you’re up against the FBI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.300
and your own fingerprint expert&nbsp;
says your fingerprints are a match?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.580
The first arrest of an American in connection&nbsp;
with the Madrid train bombings… what led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.680
the FBI to Portland, Oregon? Supporters of&nbsp;
the man say agents are on the wrong track.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:45.540
In Spain, they call it 11M. On March 11, 2004,&nbsp;
just 3 days before Spain’s general elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:53.880
explosions ripped through trains in Madrid.&nbsp;
193 people were killed and about 2,000 were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.500
injured. Europe hadn’t experienced&nbsp;
an attack on that scale in decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.840
and it came in the wake of 9/11 and at&nbsp;
the peak of the global war on terror.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.600
Fingerprints were found on a bag that contained&nbsp;
detonating devices, and Spanish authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.840
put them into the Interpol database. The&nbsp;
FBI in the United States found a match to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.800
Latent Fingerprint 17, and in May, Brandon&nbsp;
Mayfield, a U.S. Army veteran and family law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.680
attorney who frequently worked with low-income&nbsp;
clients in Portland, Oregon, took the blame.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:36.720
How does a lawyer 5,403 miles away&nbsp;
from a crime become its prime suspect?

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:43.380
To understand what went wrong here, we&nbsp;
need to go all the way back to Darwin.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:49.200
Throughout the 1800’s, scientists advanced the&nbsp;
notion that a pattern of epidermal ridges on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.980
human fingertips, toes, and palms were unique&nbsp;
to each human, and they could be used as a proof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:03.060
of one’s identity -- like a built-in biological&nbsp;
signature. Scottish surgeon Henry Faulds proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.460
that they could be cataloged and recorded&nbsp;
with ink, and he asked Charles Darwin for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:14.760
help. Darwin was a bit too old to undertake a&nbsp;
real analysis, so he passed it onto his cousin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.280
Francis Galton -- whose many accomplishments&nbsp;
included identifying the statistical regression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.220
to the mean and inventing a physical board to&nbsp;
demonstrate it that we now see on game shows.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.920
The science around fingerprint identification&nbsp;
flourished, and by the beginning of the 20th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.200
century, it had become one of the best ways&nbsp;
to pinpoint criminals -- and especially repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:44.820
offenders. It replaced systems like branding&nbsp;
skin, which France outlawed in 1832. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.140
much more scientific than Alphonse&nbsp;
Bertillon’s anthropometric forensics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:55.080
a measurement of physical factors including head&nbsp;
length, head breadth, length of middle finger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.660
length of the left foot, eye color and&nbsp;
hair color -- by assigning values for each,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.900
Bertillon claimed each person would have&nbsp;
a unique combination of data. Except… it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:13.140
wasn’t all that unique, since so many physical&nbsp;
attributes correlate with one another. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:21.180
most tall people also have long arms, you don’t&nbsp;
find many 5 foot 3 men with 7-foot wingspans.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.480
Fingerprints were much more accurate,&nbsp;
and Bertllon developed a system of 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:33.360
identifiable markers that remained standard&nbsp;
for over a century. Starting in 1924,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.940
the US government began compiling and&nbsp;
cataloging fingerprints, and by 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:45.240
they had about 200 million on file. A wavelet&nbsp;
scalar quantization algorithm was employed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:52.440
compress the 500 pixel-per-inch images down&nbsp;
to a file size of just 500kb -- which meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:59.100
easier storage, quicker access, and smoother&nbsp;
online transfers globally. Like to Spain.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.860
And by that time, it had been a century since&nbsp;
Francis Galton’s forgotten warning that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:12.300
“A complex pattern [like fingerprints] is capable&nbsp;
of suggesting various readings, as the figuring on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.520
a wallpaper may suggest a variety of forms&nbsp;
and faces to those who have such fancies.”

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.980
Even though the prints sent&nbsp;
from Spain were low quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.820
an FBI examiner signed an affidavit stating that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:33.060
“the FBI lab stands by their conclusion&nbsp;
of a 100 percent positive identification.”

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.320
In the weeks leading up to his arrest,&nbsp;
Brandon felt like he was being watched…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:47.160
because he was literally being watched. FBI&nbsp;
documents show that as soon as Brandon’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:53.340
fingerprints popped as a potential match, he&nbsp;
was immediately under 24-hour FBI surveillance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:07:00.480
including phone and digital monitoring. He also&nbsp;
suspected break-ins at his home and his office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.200
because… they were both really broken into.&nbsp;
They were convinced Mayfied was involved.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.960
Here’s a question! If fingerprints&nbsp;
are so foolproof, why was an innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.460
man harassed by the government and&nbsp;
detained for weeks as he wondered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:21.360
why they thought he was connected&nbsp;
to a crime on another continent?

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:28.680
Spanish authorities actually knew Mayfield’s print&nbsp;
wasn’t a match -- before the FBI detained him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:35.820
They communicated a result of “Negativo” after&nbsp;
their own fingerprint experts found too few points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:42.960
matching in the prints. So what’d the FBI do? The&nbsp;
FBI flew to Madrid and told them they were wrong.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:49.200
As they investigated Mayfield, they found that&nbsp;
he’d converted to Islam. They also noted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:56.040
a year prior to 11M, he’d represented a man&nbsp;
accused of involvement in a terror plot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:01.080
Oregon. The more they looked into his life,&nbsp;
the more circumstantial evidence they found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:06.780
that suggested Mayfield had the means and&nbsp;
motive to play a role in the attack -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.640
that’s actually the punchline to a&nbsp;
joke about a lying Texas sharpshooter.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:18.180
The joke’s about a man who shoots at the side of&nbsp;
a barn, and then draws a target around a cluster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:24.420
of bullet holes to suggest that he’s actually a&nbsp;
great shooter. When that’s done with data, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:31.200
called the “multiple comparisons problem.” When&nbsp;
we falsely perceive connections in our heads, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:37.380
“apophenia.” And when you’re fitting evidence to&nbsp;
a predetermined conclusion, that has a name too.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:43.020
Psychologist Peter Wason coined the&nbsp;
term “confirmation bias” to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.600
one’s tendency to focus on evidence that&nbsp;
supports their beliefs and to ignore or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.840
reject evidence that contradicts it. Basically,&nbsp;
it’s cherry-picking the truth, and it almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:01.380
guarantees that you’ll find exactly what you’re&nbsp;
looking for -- even if that isn’t reality.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.140
SO WHAT WAS THE REALITY?

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.160
An FBI report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:16.080
years after the incident admitted&nbsp;
that Mayfield was actually one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:21.360
20 potential matches to the LF-17&nbsp;
fingerprint. Once they looked into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.500
Mayfield’s life and found details&nbsp;
that supported their suspicions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:31.140
he became the prime suspect. Because he served&nbsp;
in the military, he probably had the knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:39.000
to be involved. His tangential past connections&nbsp;
to suspected terrorists were further proof. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:45.300
since he’d converted to Islam, he checked&nbsp;
almost every box on the FBI’s profile list.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.460
And remember Mayfield’s house being searched?&nbsp;
Evidence was seized from his residence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:55.140
including what the FBI called “Spanish documents.”

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:59.820
They turned out to be his son’s Spanish homework.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:06.900
Okay, okay, okay. But why did Mayfield’s own&nbsp;
defense experts agree with the FBI that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:14.520
prints were a match? They both claimed there was&nbsp;
an “unusual similarity” between the two prints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.840
with the Office of the Inspector General&nbsp;
noting that they considered the coincidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:26.520
to be “extraordinarily rare.” And now we’re&nbsp;
back to people being accused of crimes based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:33.300
on hypothetical low probability -- just like&nbsp;
Sally Clark’s tragic case in my last video.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:39.180
The FBI deliberately manipulated their&nbsp;
conclusions by ignoring that the LF-17&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:44.280
print was only a slight match with&nbsp;
Mayfield’s -- and then they focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:49.260
on evidence that supported Mayfield’s&nbsp;
guilt and ignored the parts that didn’t.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:55.380
But the FBI’s bad judgment was also a&nbsp;
function of a weird problem we have when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:01.440
we get better at collecting and using data.&nbsp;
Think of crime in Galton’s era -- there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.680
essentially no real information to work from.&nbsp;
It was very difficult to verify a suspect’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.200
identity. It was hard to find a suspect&nbsp;
given that there was no video evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:19.320
hardly any photography, and primitive&nbsp;
forensics at best. It would be generations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.960
before we could begin to collect and&nbsp;
process things like blood types and DNA.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:31.260
The FBI didn’t have too little&nbsp;
data. They actually had too much.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:36.120
They knew every little detail&nbsp;
about Brandon Mayfield’s life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:42.960
from his browser history to what was in his&nbsp;
garbage. The sheer volume of information made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.300
it easy for them to find exactly what they were&nbsp;
looking for and only what they were looking for.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:54.960
Here’s an example of how that data and knowledge&nbsp;
gap quickly goes wrong. Swedish power lines.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:01.560
In 1992, Swedish researchers concluded a&nbsp;
25-year longitudinal study to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:07.680
whether living near high-voltage power lines&nbsp;
made people sick. They studied the rates of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:15.180
over 800 different illnesses and found that&nbsp;
childhood leukemia was 4 times more common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:23.340
near power lines. Their data wasn’t wrong. The&nbsp;
problem was... by measuring so many variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:27.000
they basically guaranteed that at&nbsp;
least ONE of them would be a weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.360
statistical outlier -- and then they used it&nbsp;
as proof of a cause-and-effect relationship.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.000
You can actually visualize what this is&nbsp;
like. Look. If I have a bag of dice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.520
dump them all on my table, what’s gonna happen?&nbsp;
Most of them will form a scattered pile right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:49.020
under where I dump them. But you KNOW that&nbsp;
1 or 2 of em are gonna fly off my table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:53.220
bounce on the carpet, and lodge themselves&nbsp;
under a filing cabinet 10 feet away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:59.820
Why?!? They’re outliers, it just&nbsp;
happens. Alright, back to Mayfield.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:06.900
The FBI had a preconceived notion of who was&nbsp;
likely to have been involved in 11M. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:11.280
had seemingly-reliable forensic evidence&nbsp;
to support a suspect’s identification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.280
They had so much information about that&nbsp;
suspect, all so easily available digitally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:23.040
that they could find information that perfectly&nbsp;
fit their profile. They dismissed the high volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:29.880
of exculpatory data… like whether Brandon&nbsp;
Mayfield was even in Europe before M-11. HE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:36.900
WASN’T. His passport had expired years ago&nbsp;
-- but the FBI claimed that meant Mayfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:43.980
must’ve traveled under another identity.&nbsp;
For them, the absence of proof was proof.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:50.460
Mayfield was detained for 2 weeks as a “material&nbsp;
witness” -- NOT arrested as an actual suspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:55.920
since holding him as a material witness&nbsp;
required far less evidence. The FBI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.260
kept collecting information they thought&nbsp;
proved Mayfield’s guilt -- but everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:05.940
came crashing down when Spanish authorities&nbsp;
announced that they’d found the person who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:13.440
was actually involved in 11M. They matched the&nbsp;
LF-17 print to those of an Algerian terrorist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:20.580
and they were right. The FBI’s experts&nbsp;
agreed that these prints were truly a match.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:27.300
Mayfield was released immediately --&nbsp;
and then the questions began. As the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:33.120
American Civil Liberties Union and other civil&nbsp;
rights advocates excoriated the FBI for having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:39.840
wrongly profiled him, the FBI analyzed what&nbsp;
went wrong internally. They laid part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:45.480
blame on what they saw as the poor quality&nbsp;
of the original digital image of LF-17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:51.480
but a panel of international fingerprint&nbsp;
experts suggested that the FBI had far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:57.420
too much confidence in the Integrated Automated&nbsp;
Fingerprint Identification System, which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:05.340
largest biometric database in the world, and let&nbsp;
the pressure of a high profile case determine how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:12.420
they viewed the evidence -- and what evidence they&nbsp;
collected at all. The panel also concluded that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:19.440
“FBI culture discouraged fingerprint examiners&nbsp;
from disagreeing with their superiors.”

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:26.040
The Department of Justice released a 330-page&nbsp;
report that admitted to analytical and procedural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:32.280
errors, and academic journals churned out articles&nbsp;
analyzing the validity of fingerprint analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:39.420
the rate of false positives, and fingerprinting’s&nbsp;
role in criminal trials. Regarding the validity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:45.240
fingerprinting, a science historian at UC-Irvine&nbsp;
said, “How do you explain to the court that what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:52.380
you’ve been saying for 100 years was exaggerated,&nbsp;
but you still have something meaningful to say?”

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:58.260
Mayfield’s case led to lawsuits about potential&nbsp;
misuse and constitutionality of the Patriot Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:04.380
a $2 million judgment in his favor,&nbsp;
and a formal apology from the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:07.620
Government. Mayfield went back to practicing law.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:13.020
Nearly two decades after Mayfield’s&nbsp;
unconscionable injustice, we have even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:19.980
more data and information about everything&nbsp;
-- and about everyone. It’s amazing how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:25.620
we can know and how quickly we can know it.&nbsp;
Everything from medical records to finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:31.920
a gem mint Charizard is way faster than&nbsp;
it used to be, and usually more accurate.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:40.560
That’s generally a good thing. But… sometimes&nbsp;
all that information is a problem. Even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:47.940
it’s perfectly accurate, the way we use it&nbsp;
might not be. We can make good data bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:52.440
and it’s getting even more&nbsp;
difficult to get it right.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:57.540
Mayfield’s case highlights the complex&nbsp;
relationship between the objectivity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:02.760
science and math and the fallibility&nbsp;
of the human mind. As we advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:08.640
we find more ways to be right -- and&nbsp;
that also means more ways to be wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:16.140
The not-quite-100% reliability of fingerprint&nbsp;
analysis may have initially failed Brandon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:23.460
Mayfield, but the unchecked flaws of the&nbsp;
human mind are what landed him in a jail cell.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:27.840
Sometimes the math and science are right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:35.160
and the way we use them, the way&nbsp;
people use what’s right... is wrong.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.860
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:48.240
Vsauce! Kevin here, with a serial killer&nbsp;
who may very well have murdered hundreds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.520
Her systematic killing spree went&nbsp;
undetected for years because suspicion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:58.260
just wasn’t enough. What finally&nbsp;
brought her to justice was… math.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:15.240
In the United States, the most dangerous&nbsp;
place you can go is… the hospital. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:21.360
doctors and nurses save lives literally&nbsp;
every single day, but medical error is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:27.660
significant cause of death. No matter how hard&nbsp;
we try, tragedies happen -- and by definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:35.160
everyone in a hospital is already sick with&nbsp;
something. But murder isn’t an accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:41.820
and a nurse in Massachusetts was straight-up&nbsp;
killing patients… to impress a guy.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:46.860
Kristen Gilbert was a nurse at the Veterans&nbsp;
Administration Medical Center in Northampton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:52.920
Massachusetts. She was popular with co-workers.&nbsp;
She’d go out clubbing with them in Boston to blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.920 --> 00:19:00.420
off steam, and even threw baby showers. She also&nbsp;
seemed to be really good at her job -- it felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:07.620
like she was always around to save the day in an&nbsp;
emergency. She appeared to be the perfect nurse.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:13.320
But Gilbert was around so often&nbsp;
when patients died that other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.520
nurses jokingly referred to&nbsp;
her as the “Angel of Death.”

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:25.260
It seemed like the Northampton hospital&nbsp;
had a lot of unexpected deaths… but a VA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:31.800
investigation found that the hospital’s rate of&nbsp;
deaths was in line with similar VA hospitals.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.220
So. Case closed, right? WRONG.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:42.120
Doctors and nurses weren’t convinced, but&nbsp;
there were no witnesses who saw Gilbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:51.420
actually do anything suspicious to patients.&nbsp;
There wasn’t any evidence. But there was data.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:56.220
Here’s a question: how often did patients&nbsp;
die when Gilbert was on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.220 --> 00:20:02.640
compared to when she wasn’t? Was there a&nbsp;
correlation between her presence and patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:10.260
deaths? And if so… was it just an unlucky&nbsp;
coincidence? Or was she killing people?

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.720
The first analysis can be expressed&nbsp;
visually -- this graph shows patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:23.460
deaths in the hospital ward between 1988 and&nbsp;
1997 broken down into 3 shifts: day, evening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:30.000
and overnight. The 4pm - midnight evening&nbsp;
shift stands out as having the most deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.200
and Kristen Gilbert worked the evening shift.

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:41.160
But that doesn’t hold for every year. 1988 and&nbsp;
1989 don’t match up with the 6 years that follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:46.800
it. There weren’t that many deaths, and they were&nbsp;
pretty evenly distributed throughout the day.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:52.020
Kristen Gilbert didn’t start&nbsp;
working on the ward until 1990.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:59.400
Okay. Okay. What about the really high number of&nbsp;
deaths during overnight shifts in 1990? That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:06.480
an outlier in this whole data set -- Gilbert&nbsp;
worked evenings, but when she started in 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.600
she worked the overnight shift.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:11.220
HUH.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:16.112
HUH. HUH.

00:21:16.112 --> 00:21:22.500
But this graph is still not proof.&nbsp;
Variation in human outcomes is tremendous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:28.020
whether it’s education or physical&nbsp;
fitness or death. It’s totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:33.660
possible that Kristen Gilbert was present&nbsp;
during a statistical blip. It’s possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:38.040
there’s some nurse out there working on&nbsp;
a floor where patients never seem to die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:46.020
like some kind of Angel of Life! Just ask Brandon&nbsp;
Mayfield whether coincidences happen. Or the FBI.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:51.600
Dr. Stephen Gehlbach, who is now Dean Emeritus&nbsp;
of UMass-Amherst’s School of Public Health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:58.800
ran the numbers. He looked at 1,641 nursing&nbsp;
shifts over the period of 18 months leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:05.580
up to staff taking their concerns to hospital&nbsp;
management. There were 74 deaths during those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:13.440
shifts -- which is a death on 4.51% of&nbsp;
all shifts, or .045 deaths per shift.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:21.060
Kristen Gilbert worked 257 shifts during that&nbsp;
18 month period. With .045 deaths per shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:29.280
we’d expect her to be present for about 11 or&nbsp;
12 deaths -- about 1 every 6 weeks on average.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:37.380
15 or 16 deaths when you expect 11 or 12 could&nbsp;
be an unlucky statistical anomaly. She was there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:47.760
for 40. FORTY! A patient died on over 15% of&nbsp;
Gilbert’s shifts. What are the chances of THAT?

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:53.040
Literally 1 in 100 million -- and maybe even LESS.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:56.640
In the handwriting analysis I&nbsp;
did of Sylvia Howland’s will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:02.700
Hetty Green’s forgery matched on way too many&nbsp;
points to chalk it up to a crazy coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:08.220
1 in sextillions. Gehlbach calculated&nbsp;
p-values to estimate the chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:14.640
Gilbert’s nursing death-rate at less than 1&nbsp;
in 100 million… but we know from the Sally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:21.360
Clark trial that a probability like 1 in 73&nbsp;
million isn’t necessarily what it feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:28.020
it is. Correlation isn’t causation. Maybe&nbsp;
her patients were just sicker than normal.

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:34.980
This time, that uncertainty was okay -- it&nbsp;
was enough for the grand jury to proceed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:42.060
A grand jury doesn’t have to determine guilt or&nbsp;
innocence the way a trial does. They just decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:48.120
whether the evidence is compelling enough&nbsp;
to proceed with criminal charges. They did.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:55.080
Again. There wasn’t proof. But the math&nbsp;
gave enough context to the suspicions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.080 --> 00:24:00.000
the Veterans Administration, hospital&nbsp;
management, and Gilbert’s co-workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:07.380
to proceed with charges -- but how was&nbsp;
she killing these people? And WHY?!?

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:13.440
Prosecutors alleged that Gilbert had injected&nbsp;
patients with epinephrine – which you probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:21.060
know as adrenaline, which sped up their hearts and&nbsp;
caused cardiac failure. Adrenaline is untraceable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:25.620
but the hospital found that it had a&nbsp;
lot of epinephrine go missing during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:30.840
Gilbert’s employment. And some of&nbsp;
the patients -- like Henry Hudon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:34.380
who was relatively young and a&nbsp;
paranoid-schizophrenia patient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:42.060
weren’t likely to have 3 heart attacks in one&nbsp;
night without something artificially causing it.

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:51.840
But… why would a professional mother of 2&nbsp;
in suburbia become a deranged super-villain?

00:24:52.860 --> 00:25:00.780
Well… She seemed to have delusions of grandeur&nbsp;
in which she herself put patients in critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:07.920
condition and then heroically tried to resuscitate&nbsp;
them. She had a history of manipulative behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:14.100
mental illness, and abuse of patients&nbsp;
-- as a home care aide in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:20.640
she scalded a mentally disabled&nbsp;
child with hot bath water on purpose.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:29.160
Kristen Gilbert may have been unhinged -- but&nbsp;
she was also in love. Obsessively in love.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:36.420
Every time a patient coded, hospital policy&nbsp;
mandated that a police officer engage in CPR.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:44.400
In late 1995 and early 1996, the same police&nbsp;
officer responded to every single cardiac arrest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:53.400
that Gilbert reported: James Perrault, Kristen’s&nbsp;
boyfriend. The man she left her husband and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:59.280
children for. Witnesses noticed her touching&nbsp;
Perrault affectionately during resuscitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:06.000
efforts and wiping his sweaty brow. Court&nbsp;
papers show that "One witness will testify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:14.460
that he observed Gilbert playing `footsie' with&nbsp;
Perrault during the middle of a code.” Gross.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:20.880
Perrault ended the relationship, and&nbsp;
Gilbert was angry. On a phone call from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:26.460
a psychiatric facility, she confessed to&nbsp;
him by saying, "You know I did it. I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:32.640
it. You wanted to know. I killed those guys."&nbsp;
Perrault immediately went to the authorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:38.760
so… she called in a bomb threat to&nbsp;
the VA hospital where they worked.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:44.580
Decades of troubling behavior culminated in a&nbsp;
systematic killing spree that allowed Gilbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:52.320
to be the focal point of crisis after crisis.&nbsp;
Staff testified about her access to the adrenaline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:58.500
that induced cardiac events, and doctors&nbsp;
testified that the unexpected excess deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:07.080
were often relatively healthy, middle-aged&nbsp;
men. The same pattern just kept happening.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:13.380
Kristen Gilbert was convicted of 4 murders and&nbsp;
2 attempted murders -- and the bomb threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:19.740
a federal facility. It’s possible -- and&nbsp;
probable -- that she was involved in many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:27.060
many more patient deaths that weren’t the&nbsp;
accidents they seemed to be, but prosecutors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:33.360
couldn’t prove it. She could’ve faced the&nbsp;
death penalty, but a compassionate judge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:39.780
jury sentenced her to a life in prison without&nbsp;
the possibility of parole -- plus 20 years.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:45.240
The mathematical analysis that was so&nbsp;
critical to Gilbert’s downfall wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:51.300
even used at the trial. The trial judge ruled&nbsp;
that the statistical evidence was prejudicial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.300 --> 00:28:00.000
as it likely wouldn’t be understood by the jury as&nbsp;
NOT being iron-clad numerical proof of her guilt.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:07.740
I’ve shown that math can be an incredible asset&nbsp;
in court… or that it can turn innocent people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:14.820
into criminals. We’re getting used to scientific&nbsp;
and mathematical evidence, like DNA and forensics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:22.860
determining guilt or innocence -- but the reality&nbsp;
is math and science don’t have to do all the work.

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:30.600
Dozens, and maybe hundreds, of veterans&nbsp;
are alive in Massachusetts because of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:35.520
bar graph. Math and science don’t&nbsp;
always have to put out the fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:44.940
Sometimes all they should do — and all&nbsp;
they need to do — is sound the alarm.

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.180
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:57.840
Vsauce! Kevin here -- Jennifer Thompson&nbsp;
studied every detail of her attacker's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:04.080
face as she was being sexually assaulted at&nbsp;
knifepoint. His hairline, broad cheekbones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:09.600
a mustache that looked like a pencil-thin dirt&nbsp;
smear. She couldn't fight back physically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:15.660
but MENTALLY she could memorize every&nbsp;
little detail and bring him to justice.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:21.360
Police described Jennifer as the best&nbsp;
witness they'd ever had. Her eyewitness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:26.100
testimony convicted Ronald Cotton,&nbsp;
and he was sentenced to life in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:31.200
plus 50 years despite no physical&nbsp;
evidence linking him to the crime.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:36.600
There was no evidence because he&nbsp;
didn't do it -- and 11 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:45.240
DNA testing proved it. So how could the "best&nbsp;
witness ever" get it completely wrong? Why do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:52.380
about 70% of DNA exonerations in the US&nbsp;
involve eyewitness misidentification?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:58.800
And how can what we've seen turn&nbsp;
into something we never saw?

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:03.720
[intro]

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:10.680
I need you to… point to the shape that rolled&nbsp;
over your foot on the evening of March 14th, 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:16.080
Okay, this example seems stupid,&nbsp;
but it shows one of the many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:22.260
many problems with witness identification.&nbsp;
Only one shape in the lineup comes close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:28.500
to the description of the suspect, the&nbsp;
choice is obvious. Only a circle can roll!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:34.380
Now this lineup requires an actual&nbsp;
identification of size and color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:40.800
But here’s a question. How do you even get&nbsp;
to a lineup? First, you need a description.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:47.100
Jennifer Thompson studied pages of ears,&nbsp;
chins, and noses so police could reconstruct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:54.180
her attacker's face using an IdentiKit. In&nbsp;
1984, that was a sketch on paper -- now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.180 --> 00:31:00.240
IdentiKit is digital. The hand-drawn facial&nbsp;
composite looked nearly perfect to her. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:04.320
the police had a picture of the suspect&nbsp;
that could be broadcast on the local news.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:05.820
Here’s the problem.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:10.860
Some witnesses are amazing -- their&nbsp;
autobiographical memory of an event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:17.640
is flawless. But then there’s the rest of&nbsp;
us, and Hugo Münsterberg wrote the book on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:25.260
how what we forget and what we don’t know&nbsp;
becomes an illusion that replaces reality.

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:31.140
In 1908, Münsterberg published “On the Witness&nbsp;
Stand,” a collection of articles and essays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:37.440
about psychological factors that affect trial&nbsp;
outcomes. What’s more persuasive than a witness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:43.860
pointing to a defendant and swearing they did&nbsp;
it? Münsterberg insisted THAT gold standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.860 --> 00:31:51.540
of evidence in a trial was actually… really&nbsp;
untrustworthy. Trials pre-date the ancient Greeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:56.820
and while we’ve always known witnesses&nbsp;
could lie, only recently do we understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:04.680
how easy it is to THINK you’re telling&nbsp;
the truth when your brain is just… wrong.

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:10.680
Münsterberg suggested that justice often&nbsp;
miscarried due to the “treachery of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:17.940
memory” -- and to prove it, he pointed the&nbsp;
finger at himself. He wasn’t just some academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:25.440
pontificating on criminal justice theory -- he&nbsp;
was robbed, and as a witness, he messed it all up.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:32.700
It went like this: He testified under oath that&nbsp;
burglars entered his house through a window. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:39.840
actually came in through the cellar. He said they&nbsp;
left a clock packed in paper. It was wrapped in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:47.340
tablecloth. He gave a complete list of garments&nbsp;
that were stolen; it turns out they took 7 more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:54.180
than he said. He claimed he found candle wax on&nbsp;
the 2nd floor… but the wax drippings were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.180 --> 00:33:02.760
in the attic. Finally, he reported 2 burglars.&nbsp;
But maybe it was just 1 -- he had no way to tell.

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:10.620
Münsterberg had given over 3,000 academic lectures&nbsp;
and not once had to use note cards. His mind and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:17.520
memory were amazing. But he admitted that he’d&nbsp;
rushed into the house and surveyed it all quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:25.260
noting after the fact that his brain filled in&nbsp;
the gaps for him. The burglar was eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:30.300
caught breaking into another house, and they&nbsp;
found Münsterberg’s jacket in his apartment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:39.840
But the fact is -- the world’s expert on witness&nbsp;
reliability -- was almost completely wrong. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:46.200
said, “I felt myself somewhat alarmed in&nbsp;
seeing how many illusions had come in.”

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:48.300
So…

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:54.960
If the pioneer of witness reliability&nbsp;
scholarship couldn't overcome his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.960 --> 00:34:00.900
illusions, what chance did a college student&nbsp;
traumatized by a violent sexual assault have?

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:07.320
Police presented Jennifer Thompson with a photo&nbsp;
lineup of 6 men. They told her that the suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:12.840
might not even be in the lineup -- but she&nbsp;
figured if he wasn’t there, why would they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:19.680
bother? She recognized one of the photos and&nbsp;
chose Ronald Cotton with absolute certainty.

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:25.140
A week later, Jennifer recognized the&nbsp;
same man in a physical lineup. To her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.140 --> 00:34:31.020
the parts that didn’t match her memory made&nbsp;
him even guiltier -- like he’d changed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:37.560
hair to purposely deceive her. The photo lineup&nbsp;
reinforced her mental image of her attacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:44.220
and anti-proof became proof. Police&nbsp;
confirmed that she’d chosen the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.220 --> 00:34:51.660
man in both lineups. She said, "It was&nbsp;
him. There was no doubt in my mind."

00:34:51.660 --> 00:34:55.380
Did you feel absolutely certain?&nbsp;
Absolutely certain. Well, what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.380 --> 00:35:00.360
said to me afterwards was, “That’s the same&nbsp;
person you picked out in the photo lineup.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:05.793
So in my mind I thought, “Bingo! I did it right!”

00:35:05.793 --> 00:35:09.000
So if that’s the case… How can&nbsp;
the truth compete with that?

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:16.140
“Estimator variables” are the parts of eyewitness&nbsp;
identification that police can’t control. So like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.140 --> 00:35:23.820
cross-racial ID’s tend to be less accurate&nbsp;
than ID’s within a witness’s race. That isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:29.220
the police’s fault. People think different&nbsp;
things, and they just plain see different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:38.340
things -- from an optical illusion to the colors&nbsp;
of a dress. But the real enemy of memory is time.

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:43.740
Hermann Ebbinghaus’s forgetting curve&nbsp;
provides a visual representation of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:48.840
quickly your memory can erode -- and&nbsp;
as early as the 1880’s his equation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:55.140
expressed the vulnerability of memory&nbsp;
over time. But forgetting over time is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.140 --> 00:36:00.900
only half the problem -- the other&nbsp;
half is teaching yourself the lie.

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:07.080
Every time you re-tell your story to police,&nbsp;
to a lawyer, to a journalist, to a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:14.160
you’re essentially engaging in the spaced&nbsp;
repetition of practice. You’re learning. Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:20.700
memories get clearer, almost like memorizing&nbsp;
lines for a play. So the longer it’s been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:25.980
the more you forget. The more you forget,&nbsp;
the more you invent. The more you repeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:31.320
the more confident you are&nbsp;
-- whether it’s true or not.

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:35.160
Jennifer Thompson said, “If&nbsp;
there was a death penalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:41.940
I wanted Ronald Cotton to get it. I would pull&nbsp;
the switch if they let me.” When she pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:47.460
her finger at Cotton to identify him during&nbsp;
the trial, she said she wished it had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:55.080
a gun. Ronald Cotton had become her attacker&nbsp;
in her mind through unconscious transference.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:37:00.960
But there was no physical evidence -- no&nbsp;
fingerprints, no clothing, no fibers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:07.020
the victim’s apartment -- linking Ronald Cotton&nbsp;
to the crime. He maintained his innocence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:13.680
refused to entertain a plea bargain. He was&nbsp;
convicted based largely on Jennifer’s testimony.

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:18.480
3 years later, Jennifer Thompson came face to face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:23.220
with her actual attacker&nbsp;
-- and didn’t even notice.

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:30.540
Ronald Cotton was given a new trial -- that’s&nbsp;
great, right? WRONG -- this time he also faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:37.440
charges for another assault and burglary from&nbsp;
that same night. Meanwhile, he’d met a man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:44.400
prison who looked a lot like Jennifer’s original&nbsp;
composite sketch -- and he looked so similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:50.700
Ronald Cotton that other inmates couldn’t tell&nbsp;
them apart. Bobby Poole lived in the same town as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:56.760
Ronald and Jennifer, and he was currently serving&nbsp;
a sentence for sexual assault. If all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.760 --> 00:38:03.060
wasn’t enough to raise suspicions, he’d literally&nbsp;
confessed to other inmates that he assaulted her.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:09.840
Ronald’s lawyers called Bobby Poole as&nbsp;
a witness in the new trial. Poole denied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:15.600
everything. Jennifer Thompson had wanted to be&nbsp;
the one to personally execute Ronald Cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:22.980
but when she was in the same courtroom as her&nbsp;
real attacker, the man whose face she’d studied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:31.260
she didn’t recognize him at all. Ronald was&nbsp;
convicted again and sent back to prison.

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:37.260
Richard Rosen, a professor at UNC-Chapel&nbsp;
Hill, and lawyer Tom Lambeth took on Ronald’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:44.160
case. They secured the DNA analysis that&nbsp;
wasn’t available in 1984. Ronald insisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:51.120
that he'd be cleared -- and the stakes were&nbsp;
high. A DNA match would cement his guilt.

00:38:51.780 --> 00:38:59.460
Ronald wasn’t lying. The tests showed that not&nbsp;
only was Ronald innocent, but that Bobby Poole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:07.260
was the attacker -- and over 10 years after&nbsp;
first being arrested, Ronald Cotton was free.

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:15.180
Jennifer didn’t dispute the DNA findings. She&nbsp;
knew she’d been proven wrong. She wasn’t defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:21.720
about it; she was horrified. She said, “I had&nbsp;
to tell people not only was I was a rape victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:27.960
but a rape victim with an extremely bad memory&nbsp;
and someone had to pay 11 years for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:31.980
I wondered how I could have been so stupid.”

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:39.840
Jennifer was not stupid. During the attack she&nbsp;
actually convinced Poole to get rid of his knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:45.240
then outwitted him by offering to get him a&nbsp;
drink and using the distraction to escape.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:53.880
She just fell prey to the vulnerabilities&nbsp;
all witnesses have and a few simple changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:57.660
can drastically improve witness reliability.

00:39:57.660 --> 00:40:05.040
Lineups that offer a range of similar choices&nbsp;
are most effective -- that way suspects can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:12.060
stand out. And making lineups sequential, so&nbsp;
one photo at a time is compared to a witness’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:18.960
memory instead of being compared to one another,&nbsp;
is even more effective. Police need to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.960 --> 00:40:24.240
clear to witnesses that the suspect might not&nbsp;
be there -- and the cops who make the lineup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:29.460
shouldn’t be the ones who administer it.&nbsp;
That ‘double blind’ process ensures police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:34.620
can’t give any verbal or non-verbal cues&nbsp;
to the witness to influence their choice.

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:37.440
Recording the lineup solidifies the record,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:43.140
and police should ask witnesses to detail&nbsp;
their level of confidence in their choice.

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:46.380
But Jennifer Thompson would’ve said 100%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:53.400
and advancing science was the only thing that&nbsp;
saved Ronald Cotton from a life in prison.

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:56.940
“In my mind, I was sure of what I saw.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:04.320
Um. Thinking back on it, uh, I don’t know&nbsp;
that I would’ve done anything different.”

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:11.160
Jennifer and Ronald are friends&nbsp;
now. He never blamed her for being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:14.280
wrong. They’ve given talks together nationwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:20.280
their families are close, and they even end&nbsp;
phone conversations by saying “Love you.”

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:27.360
Half the states in the US have instituted some&nbsp;
form of eyewitness identification reform. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:34.200
can we make sure what happened to both Ronald&nbsp;
Cotton and Jennifer Thompson never happens again?

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:42.360
Probably not. Hugo Münsterberg was right --&nbsp;
from biological limitations to systematic error,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:49.260
the moment our eyes and ears capture&nbsp;
something, the truth is vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:53.160
We can get better, but we'll never be perfect.

00:41:54.600 --> 00:42:03.300
2,200 years ago, the Roman playwright Plautus said&nbsp;
that “1 eyewitness is worth 10 ear witnesses.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:08.520
People attest to the truth by saying&nbsp;
they saw something with their own two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:14.400
eyes. They’ll tell you that something is&nbsp;
so good you need to see it to believe it.

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:20.760
But the truth is… sometimes you&nbsp;
need to believe it to see it.

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:23.700
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:33.660
Vsauce! Kevin here -- with the dumbest&nbsp;
math ever used to convict an innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.660 --> 00:42:38.760
person in a court of law. It all started&nbsp;
with a little old lady in an alleyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:44.940
and it ended by defining how we use&nbsp;
probabilistic evidence in trials, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:52.920
the California Supreme court warning that bad math&nbsp;
could become a “veritable sorcerer” to ruin law.

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:59.220
Almost 60 years ago, a petty neighborhood&nbsp;
robbery you've never heard of changed everything.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:10.020
An elderly woman named Juanita Brooks was walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:14.640
down an alleyway when someone shoved&nbsp;
her to the ground. She looked up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:19.740
see a blonde woman running away -- and&nbsp;
that woman had taken Juanita’s purse.

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:25.740
At the end of the alley, a man named John Bass&nbsp;
witnessed a white blonde woman with a ponytail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:33.360
get into a yellow car and drive past him. He saw&nbsp;
that the driver was a black man with a beard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:39.600
a mustache. These SOUND LIKE the basic facts of&nbsp;
the case -- but they ended up becoming numbers.

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:46.200
And it happened because of total uncertainty.&nbsp;
John Bass saw that the woman had a ponytail;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:51.840
Juanita Brooks couldn’t remember if she&nbsp;
did. A police officer asked around local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:56.580
gas stations until he found someone who&nbsp;
said that an interracial couple filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:02.160
up their yellow car there frequently --&nbsp;
Janet Collins and her husband Malcolm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:07.800
They’d only been married for 2 weeks, and&nbsp;
now they were being arrested for robbery.

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:13.200
When Juanita Brooks and John Bass were asked&nbsp;
to identify Janet and Malcolm from photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:20.400
neither one could do it. Juanita completely&nbsp;
blanked, and Bass only saw a resemblance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:26.520
the ponytail. The police still had some&nbsp;
case -- their alibi was a little shaky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:33.180
Malcolm had a criminal record, and a recorded&nbsp;
conversation with police showed Janet’s desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:39.300
to take the blame to protect her husband.&nbsp;
And that’s when desperation kicked in.

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:46.380
The prosecution turned Juanita Brooks’&nbsp;
assault and theft into a probability problem.

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:52.260
Prosecutor Ray Sinetar brought in a professor&nbsp;
from Cal State Long Beach to help him prove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:58.680
to the jury mathematically that Janet and&nbsp;
Malcolm Collins were guilty. The professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:03.900
testified to the validity of the product rule&nbsp;
-- which is that if events are independent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:09.480
you can multiply the probabilities of each to&nbsp;
find the chance that they happen together. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:15.180
example, if Thing A has a 1 in 5 chance of&nbsp;
happening, and Thing B has a 1 in 10 chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:22.140
of happening, then both of them happening&nbsp;
separately-but-together is 1 in 50, or 2%.

00:45:22.140 --> 00:45:27.960
So what did Sinetar do next? He&nbsp;
started literally making up numbers.

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:33.720
Sinetar invented 6 vague estimates&nbsp;
touching on the only details that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:39.360
John Bass and Juanita Brooks could provide.&nbsp;
Here’s what he said. He said the chances of:

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:45.060
-- A black man having a beard were 1 out of 10.
-- That a man would have a mustache? 1 out of 4.&nbsp;

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:50.460
-- That a white woman had blonde hair: 1 out of 3.
-- That a woman had a ponytail:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.740
1 out of 10.
-- That an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:59.460
interracial couple would be in a car: 1 in 1,000.
-- And that the car would be yellow? 1 in 10.

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:04.920
Sure. The professor was given a&nbsp;
pencil and paper to work out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.920 --> 00:46:10.980
probability of all those 6 events happening&nbsp;
together using the product rule, and he did:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:20.580
the answer was 1 in 12,000,000. And that&nbsp;
was it. He left the witness stand. Yeah.

00:46:20.580 --> 00:46:26.400
If you watched my video about Sally Clark,&nbsp;
you know exactly what’s happening here.

00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:30.900
The prosecutor explained to the jury&nbsp;
that this sort of mathematical proof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:35.880
was actually better than the concept&nbsp;
of proof beyond a reasonable doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:43.920
because it dealt with hard numbers instead of&nbsp;
vague beliefs. He called it the “new math,” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:48.780
he said it would help convict the people&nbsp;
who “push old ladies down and take their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:55.500
money. “ He said that Malcolm and Janet Collins&nbsp;
were the only two people in Los Angeles who could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.500 --> 00:47:02.760
satisfy all the elements of probability in&nbsp;
this case. He suggested that they had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:09.780
the 1 in 12 million -- because with all those&nbsp;
pieces matching up, how could they NOT be?

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:18.900
But this wasn’t new math. It was bad math. And&nbsp;
it was still enough to convict the Collinses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.900 --> 00:47:23.100
of second degree robbery. And&nbsp;
in Jim Crow-era Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.100 --> 00:47:27.900
there was almost certainly an&nbsp;
element of racial bias supporting it.

00:47:27.900 --> 00:47:33.780
Malcolm Collins appealed his conviction, and&nbsp;
it went to California's Supreme Court -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.780 --> 00:47:37.560
they finally obliterated&nbsp;
the prosecutor’s stupidity.

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:44.580
The math was obviously wrong -- starting&nbsp;
with the completely made-up odds for each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:48.720
characteristic. It’s not like the&nbsp;
prosecutor analyzed the vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:54.420
registrations of all California automobiles&nbsp;
to determine that 10% of the state’s cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.420 --> 00:48:00.420
were yellow. He didn’t conduct a massive&nbsp;
survey of hairdressers to find out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.420 --> 00:48:05.640
percentage of their clients pulled their&nbsp;
hair back into a pontail. He just guessed.

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:11.640
Then he failed to acknowledge that these&nbsp;
events are NOT independent -- like beards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:17.700
and mustaches. Ya know, they kinda tend&nbsp;
to come as a pair… a lot. Not always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:23.640
but a lot. So, you can’t just straight&nbsp;
up multiply them like they’re coin flips.

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:30.240
But here’s the big one. Let’s say the math&nbsp;
is accurate -- which it isn’t. You’ve found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:37.320
a couple who satisfy all the conditions to be&nbsp;
the 1 in 12 million. Great. How do you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:43.980
THEY are the couple who committed the crime?!&nbsp;
Are they the only two people in the greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:50.040
Los Angeles metropolitan area who were a blonde&nbsp;
ponytail and black mustached-and-bearded couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:56.460
in a yellow car?? Surely there weren’t MANY&nbsp;
-- but there’s a decent chance there’s a few.

00:48:56.460 --> 00:49:01.740
The Supreme Court actually calculated&nbsp;
the probability that another couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:11.640
fitting the same exact description at over&nbsp;
40%. 40%! So even if the math checked out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.640 --> 00:49:17.040
there’s pretty close to even odds that&nbsp;
Janet and Malcolm were the wrong couple.

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:23.280
3 years later, Malcolm’s conviction was&nbsp;
overturned and he was released -- Janet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:28.320
had been given a short sentence, and&nbsp;
she’d already served it. The Collinses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:34.380
never really got justice. Janet served&nbsp;
her time and Malcolm lost a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:40.260
of his life -- but the biggest loser was&nbsp;
the validity of probabilistic evidence.

00:49:40.260 --> 00:49:46.800
It’s the most glaring example of the&nbsp;
prosecutor’s fallacy -- and it’s taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:53.340
decades to undo the damage Ray Sinetar&nbsp;
caused to the use of math in the courtroom.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:50:00.300
But the fallout wasn’t ALL bad. It did lead&nbsp;
to the “Collins test,” a set of conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.300 --> 00:50:06.600
the introduction of probability needs to satisfy&nbsp;
for the math to be relevant and valid in a trial:

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:12.240
1) The probability factors need to be&nbsp;
accurate -- you can’t just make them up;&nbsp;

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:18.780
2) The calculations are done correctly and&nbsp;
formulas are applied appropriately -- like…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:22.260
not abusing the product rule&nbsp;
to get the result you want;&nbsp;

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:28.680
3) All the factors being calculated&nbsp;
actually apply to the parties involved,&nbsp;

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:34.260
4) And the evidence proves there&nbsp;
could be only ONE result -- so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:40.620
that the Collinses could be the ONLY possible&nbsp;
couple capable of satisfying every condition.

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:48.720
The police never did figure out who knocked&nbsp;
Juanita Brooks down and stole her purse. In 1965,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:57.660
two criminals in Los Angeles got $40 richer, and&nbsp;
math got a lot less trustworthy in the courtroom.

00:50:57.660 --> 00:51:03.900
But without it we don’t have the Collins&nbsp;
test, or an ever-evolving debate about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.900 --> 00:51:10.440
we can leverage what we know about math to find&nbsp;
out what we don’t know about everything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:20.760
Proving that sometimes things have to get really&nbsp;
dumb before we can even start to get… smart.

00:51:21.540 --> 00:51:23.700
And as always - thanks for watching.

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:28.800
Vsauce!

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:31.680
Kevin here -- Kevin Lieber, actually.

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:33.960
That’s my name, it’s German.

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:40.140
But I’m not the only one -- there are 10
other Kevin Liebers in the United States.

00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:47.760
So… what if one of the other Kevin Liebers
is connected to a murder… of another YouTuber…

00:51:47.760 --> 00:51:50.340
3 miles from right here.

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:56.580
And the only surviving victim identified ME,
Kevin Lieber, as the killer?

00:51:56.580 --> 00:52:02.640
To top it all off, Mr Beast testified that
I confessed to him?

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:07.920
And only one who knows I’m innocent!?

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:10.560
All of this really happened.

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:14.820
Not to me -- to a woman named Joyce Ann Brown.

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:42.780
They need a conviction.

00:52:42.780 --> 00:52:44.520
They needed the pressure off.

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:46.200
Two blacks in Dallas, Texas?

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:47.460
In Texas period?

00:52:47.460 --> 00:52:52.260
Walked in broad open daylight… show and
killed a business man?

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:53.700
Somebody was gonna pay.

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:55.200
I’m that somebody.

00:52:55.200 --> 00:53:01.680
On Thursday, May 8th, 1980, the Dallas Morning
News published a short article buried in the

00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:07.920
paper’s Local News section -- a shop called
Fine Furs by Rubin had been robbed and its

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:10.680
owner, Rubin Danziger, was murdered.

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:18.480
His wife Ala said Rubin pleaded, “Don’t
hurt me, don’t hurt me,” before one of

00:53:18.480 --> 00:53:22.260
two female robbers shot him in the head.

00:53:22.260 --> 00:53:27.780
Ala escaped by telling them she had terminal
cancer with just weeks to live.

00:53:27.780 --> 00:53:32.820
One of the women said, “We’ll just let
you suffer,” and left.

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:38.880
As police looked for suspects, they found
the getaway car -- a Datsun rented to a woman

00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:41.100
named Joyce Ann Brown.

00:53:41.100 --> 00:53:46.920
A police officer recognized the name as someone
who’d been arrested years prior for a minor

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:53.100
sex work charge, so they asked publicly for
Joyce Ann Brown to come in for questioning.

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:57.840
Joyce immediately went into the police station
to clear up the confusion.

00:53:57.840 --> 00:54:03.480
She wasn’t worried -- she knew she didn’t
do it and she had an alibi.

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:06.540
She was at work when the crime occurred.

00:54:06.540 --> 00:54:12.780
And this is where the incredible coincidences
really begin.

00:54:12.780 --> 00:54:20.160
Joyce worked at Koslow’s, another fur store
just across town, so she’d presumably know

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:22.500
which valuable furs to steal.

00:54:22.500 --> 00:54:29.400
Working nearby in the fur industry with the
same first, middle and last name as the getaway

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:30.480
car’s renter?

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:31.980
Quite a coincidence.

00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:32.700
But.

00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:36.240
Even so, Joyce denied any involvement.

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:41.640
The interrogator told her, ​​“You’re
lying, we know you pulled that robbery.”

00:54:42.540 --> 00:54:46.620
She was arrested and held on $1 million bail.

00:54:46.620 --> 00:54:53.580
Then police realized that the Joyce Ann Brown
who rented the car actually lived in Denver,

00:54:53.580 --> 00:54:58.980
Colorado -- she was a completely different
person in a completely different state -- and

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:04.920
THAT Joyce said that she’d lent the car
to a friend -- but by then it was too late.

00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:11.700
Joyce’s mugshot from her prior arrest had
been shown in a lineup to Mrs. Danziger, who

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:18.300
identified her as one of the robbers and she
said that that woman had been in the store

00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:21.900
to case it out in the weeks leading up to
the crime.

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:24.780
Forget a weird name thing.

00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:28.500
Now the police had an eyewitness identification.

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:32.640
Okay but what about Joyce’s solid work alibi?

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:39.180
There was about a 7-minute window that would’ve
allowed Joyce to commit the robbery, leave

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:44.280
the scene, change her clothes, drive back
to work, and compose herself as if nothing

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:50.820
was out of the ordinary before talking to
a co-worker at 1:19pm -- and the police said

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:53.460
that must be exactly what happened.

00:55:54.060 --> 00:55:56.280
But what ACTUALLY happened?

00:55:56.280 --> 00:56:03.000
None of the items recovered from police searches
-- the rental car, Joyce’s apartment -- linked

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:03.900
her to the crime.

00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:10.860
But when evidence starts to suggest that weird
things are coincidences, it’s tempting to…

00:56:10.860 --> 00:56:13.140
just come up with new evidence.

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:19.560
Suddenly a woman named Martha Jean Bruce claimed
that Joyce had confessed to her while they

00:56:19.560 --> 00:56:22.080
shared a jail cell after Joyce’s arrest.

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:28.740
Bruce said there was no incentive for her
to testify to that, but remarkably a few months

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:35.640
later the DA recommended that Bruce be released
over 3 years early on a 5-year attempted murder

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:36.240
sentence.

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:38.280
So yeah.

00:56:38.280 --> 00:56:42.150
The DA’s investigator Adi Christian found
her story convincing.

00:56:42.150 --> 00:56:47.040
Well, after I talked to her a little bit in
detail she began to tell me some things that

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:51.060
indicated to me that she would’ve had to
have talked to someone involved in that crime

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:52.620
because of the facts she had.

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:57.960
About the way the furs were supposedly baggaged
when they left the scene.

00:56:57.960 --> 00:57:01.920
And Martha Jean Bruce had that information,
she knew that they were in plastic bags.

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:04.020
But she could’ve read that in the newspapers.

00:57:04.020 --> 00:57:09.420
Oh, and Martha Jean Bruce had already been
convicted of lying to police.

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:12.660
But the prosecutors ignored that.

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:19.740
The odds of it all being the universe’s
cruel joke against Joyce Ann Brown were just...

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:21.120
too much for the jury.

00:57:21.120 --> 00:57:27.960
She was found guilty and sentenced -- and
there was no hope the calculus was ever gonna

00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:29.220
be in her favor.

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:34.500
Everything added up for the prosecution, and
the pattern checked out.

00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:41.040
Their case went like this: Joyce Ann Brown
is the black former prostitute in Texas accused

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:45.420
of involvement in the robbery and murder of
a holocaust survivor on her lunch break from

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:47.580
work at the same type of store a few miles&nbsp;

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:51.060
away, with the surviving&nbsp;
victim visually identifying

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:57.180
her, sworn testimony of a jailhouse confession,
and the getaway car rented in her exact name.

00:57:58.020 --> 00:57:58.920
Here’s a question.

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:04.920
What are the odds someone with YOUR name commits
a crime?

00:58:04.920 --> 00:58:09.900
Since there are only 10 other Kevin Liebers,
I’m pretty safe.

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:11.760
But what if your name is…

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:13.500
Michael Stevens?

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:19.980
There are 2,975 total Michael Stevenses in
the United States.

00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:22.440
How about John Smith?

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:29.160
He’s got 48,439 other potential criminal
John Smiths.

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:35.280
Conversely, Tanner Butt’s innocence is firmly
up to him.

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:42.180
6 months after Joyce’s conviction, the other
woman linked to the robbery and murder was

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:43.920
finally apprehended.

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:45.540
Rene Taylor.

00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:49.860
Police found furs and the murder weapon in
her apartment.

00:58:49.860 --> 00:58:56.700
She told police that she had no idea who Joyce
Ann Brown was… but she refused to give up

00:58:56.700 --> 00:59:02.280
the name of her actual accomplice because
she didn’t want to ruin that woman’s life.

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:08.460
Rene Taylor was convicted of murder and sent
to the Mountain View maximum security prison,

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:10.620
where she served time with…

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:12.120
Joyce Ann Brown.

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:16.200
Guards were ordered to keep the two women
apart.

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:18.120
Joyce is in prison.

00:59:18.120 --> 00:59:20.100
Rene Taylor is in prison.

00:59:20.100 --> 00:59:27.780
But It took years to determine who Rene’s
real accomplice was -- a woman named Lorraine

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:33.960
Germany who’d also robbed an Albuquerque,
New Mexico fur store with Taylor -- and an

00:59:33.960 --> 00:59:41.340
investigator told Joyce that “Lorraine looked
enough like you to be your twin sister.”

00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:42.720
Name.

00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:43.800
Furs.

00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:44.760
DOPPELGANGER.

00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:46.260
Ridiculous.

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:51.840
In 1984, Joyce’s appeal was denied on her
birthday.

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:55.440
2 weeks later, her son shot himself.

00:59:55.440 --> 01:00:00.420
His last words to her were, “Mama, when
are they going to let you out?

01:00:00.420 --> 01:00:02.400
We don’t have a family anymore.”

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:09.540
Most crimes aren’t all THAT different from
each other, and the details usually add up.

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:12.960
There are often familiar patterns.

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:19.620
The Dallas police fell victim to the Einstellung
Effect, a cognitive phenomenon detailed in

01:00:19.620 --> 01:00:21.960
my video "Can Learning Make You Dumb?"

01:00:21.960 --> 01:00:25.380
They had tunnel vision based on their experience.

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:32.400
They also had a lot of possible evidence to
choose from, so they only saw what they wanted

01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:36.600
to see -- and they wanted to see it in a certain
way.

01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:41.280
It’s the same mistake we saw with Brandon
Mayfield’s fingerprint case.

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:49.980
And just like Sally Clark’s trial, the prosecution
and jury refused to admit that long odds and

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:55.080
crazy coincidences really do happen -- they
just do.

01:00:55.080 --> 01:00:59.280
Or maybe they just didn’t understand that
they do.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:05.400
One way to combat the Einstellung Effect is
to bring in people to look at the problem

01:01:05.400 --> 01:01:09.060
who aren’t used to operating in familiar
patterns.

01:01:09.060 --> 01:01:14.400
Dallas Morning News journalists Steve McGonigal
and Steve Blow started questioning the district

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:15.540
attorney’s narrative.

01:01:15.540 --> 01:01:19.080
Jim McCloskey of Centurion&nbsp;
Ministries in Princeton,&nbsp;

01:01:19.080 --> 01:01:21.720
New Jersey and his own investigator started

01:01:21.720 --> 01:01:25.740
putting the evidence together to get Joyce
exonerated.

01:01:25.740 --> 01:01:33.480
And in 1989, just weeks after Morley Safer
of 60 Minutes aired a 14-minute piece detailing

01:01:33.480 --> 01:01:39.060
the doubt surrounding Joyce’s conviction,
she was finally granted a retrial.

01:01:39.060 --> 01:01:48.600
For the first time in 9 years, 5 months, and
24 days, Joyce was free -- and months later,

01:01:48.600 --> 01:01:52.080
the DA dropped the case against her.

01:01:52.080 --> 01:01:57.420
Joyce Ann Brown spent the years following
her release working as an assistant to a county

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:03.240
official before founding an organization to
assist the incarcerated and their families.

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:06.360
She died of a heart attack in 2015.

01:02:06.360 --> 01:02:14.580
Joyce’s strength and character allowed her
to turn her injustice into a springboard to

01:02:14.580 --> 01:02:15.540
help other people.

01:02:15.540 --> 01:02:20.460
But… she never should’ve had to in the
first place.

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:25.980
Joyce wrote in her book “Justice Denied”
that she, “... had faith in the system.

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:29.640
If you did a crime, you did the time.

01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:32.340
If you were innocent, you went free.”

01:02:32.340 --> 01:02:39.900
The thing is -- lives like Joyce Ann Brown’s
have been and will continue to be destroyed

01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:43.260
because we keep getting the same things wrong.

01:02:43.260 --> 01:02:52.020
We fall prey to the same cognitive biases,
analytical fallacies, and just plain thinking

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:56.100
errors that I’ve been exploring on this
channel for years.

01:02:56.100 --> 01:03:02.040
One day it’s the Einstellung effect -- the
next day it’s confirmation bias, or any

01:03:02.040 --> 01:03:05.760
of dozens of misinterpretations in math.

01:03:05.760 --> 01:03:08.100
I know all of these things.

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:10.680
YOU know all of these things.

01:03:10.680 --> 01:03:15.840
But not everyone else does&nbsp;
-- and their compounding&nbsp;

01:03:15.840 --> 01:03:18.840
errors can determine the freedom of people

01:03:18.840 --> 01:03:25.320
named… well, whatever your first, middle,
and last name is.

01:03:25.320 --> 01:03:28.560
And as always, thanks for watching.

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:32.220
Well the first thing I'm gonna do is spend
some time with my daughter.

01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:35.820
And that very night I'm gonna sleep in the
bed with my momma.

01:03:36.780 --> 01:03:39.420
I've been waitin' on that day a long time.

01:03:44.700 --> 01:03:49.800
Vsauce! Kevin here -- with a math crime&nbsp;
so sinister the Michigan Supreme Court&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.800 --> 01:03:54.780
stated that no other game operated&nbsp;
more extensively or demoralized so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.780 --> 01:04:01.380
many people. City police forces repeatedly&nbsp;
shut it down, and the FBI waged all-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:01.380 --> 01:04:08.400
war against it. And it made criminals out of&nbsp;
people like… a Detroit mom named Miss Fannie.

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:11.760
What they didn’t know is that&nbsp;
they were fighting against a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:17.100
nearly perfect game that was about&nbsp;
way more than just beating the odds.

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:27.120
In Atlanta, it was called “Playing The Bug”&nbsp;
-- in Harlem and elsewhere, it was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:34.020
called “Policy” after the original game that drew&nbsp;
numbers from 1 to 78. But the real name is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:34.020 --> 01:04:40.020
“Numbers” -- and although it was associated with&nbsp;
black communities, everyone played the Numbers.

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:44.880
When Fannie Davis set up her Detroit&nbsp;
neighborhood Numbers game in 1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:51.060
there just wasn’t much economic opportunity&nbsp;
for the black community -- so, she started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:51.060 --> 01:04:57.000
her own business. And she funded it&nbsp;
after winning big on Numbers herself.

01:04:57.000 --> 01:04:58.440
It worked like this.

01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:05.220
You’d place a bet on a 3-digit number from&nbsp;
000 to 999, and that could hit one of two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:05.220 --> 01:05:09.480
ways. You could play it straight, where&nbsp;
you hoped to hit the exact number -- like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:16.980
607 -- and you’d be paid 500 or 600 times&nbsp;
your bet. Or you could bet a number “boxed,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:23.880
meaning you’d win if any combination of your&nbsp;
numbers hit. That’s 3!, so 3 x 2 x 1 = 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:23.880 --> 01:05:27.720
possible combinations [607, 670, 760, 706,&nbsp;
067, 076], and it would pay about 80 to 1.

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:30.900
Okay. So. How did they choose the winning number?

01:05:32.220 --> 01:05:40.560
I’ve got a cage full of balls numbered 1 to 100 --&nbsp;
I’ll give it a crank and then I’ll draw three. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:47.280
gonna play: 394. That’s my lucky number because&nbsp;
.394 was Tony Gwynn’s batting average in 1994&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:47.280 --> 01:05:52.560
when he was on pace to hit over .400 for the&nbsp;
first time since Ted Williams hit .406 in 1941,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:52.560 --> 01:05:59.520
but a labor strike shortened the baseball&nbsp;
season and RUINED EVERYTHING. Okay. Here we go…&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:10.440
32. I already lost. Oh well let’s keep going.

01:06:12.720 --> 01:06:13.620
Oh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:29.100
My cage of balls broke. Oh. I am&nbsp;
a fool. Oh there’s so many on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.100 --> 01:06:38.880
floor. Alright let’s finish this&nbsp;
off. Stay closed. 86. Last number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.100 --> 01:06:48.600
91. The winning number was 261, my number is 394.

01:06:49.440 --> 01:06:50.220
Sorry, Tony...

01:06:50.220 --> 01:06:54.360
But… this method is silly. No&nbsp;
one would trust a guy pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:54.360 --> 01:06:58.560
balls out of a cage in his house. To&nbsp;
get the winning number in a more fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.560 --> 01:07:03.900
manner… I could do something like… take&nbsp;
3 YouTube videos, reload them each day&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.900 --> 01:07:08.700
on a livestream and use the ones digit from&nbsp;
the view counts to pick the winning number.

01:07:08.700 --> 01:07:12.360
And Fannie’s game worked kinda similar to that.

01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:17.160
She used the “mutuel” from a local racetrack&nbsp;
-- the betting totals for win, place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.160 --> 01:07:22.800
and show on a series of horse races. She’d&nbsp;
take the ones digit from the 4th, 5th, and 6th&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.800 --> 01:07:29.820
race totals, and there’s your 3 numbers. It was&nbsp;
technically possible to influence those digits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:35.940
but incredibly difficult -- and expensive. Other&nbsp;
games used independent numbers like digits from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:41.400
the published daily balance of the US Treasury&nbsp;
-- things you could open up a newspaper and see.

01:07:41.400 --> 01:07:48.120
Look, here’s the real beauty of Numbers and why&nbsp;
it worked so well for Fannie and her community:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.120 --> 01:07:55.320
You win just often enough to enjoy playing,&nbsp;
and to validate every possible betting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.320 --> 01:08:00.960
system. If you play 6 days a week you’ll&nbsp;
probably win boxed 2 or 3 times a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.960 --> 01:08:07.140
and you’ll hit straight for 500 times your&nbsp;
bet every few years. Did a number come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:13.920
you in a dream? Do you think you have a scientific&nbsp;
lottery-beating system? They all end up winning.

01:08:13.920 --> 01:08:21.480
And yeah, the house edge was steep -- getting&nbsp;
paid 500 to 1 on something you know has a 1 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:21.480 --> 01:08:27.540
1,000 chance of happening means someone’s making&nbsp;
money somewhere. They’re also taking bets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.540 --> 01:08:30.180
keeping meticulous records,&nbsp;
collecting money from losses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.180 --> 01:08:34.980
and distributing money from wins -- all as&nbsp;
they’re calculating their own mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.980 --> 01:08:41.340
risk of ruin in real-time to make sure they&nbsp;
can survive financially if someone hits big.

01:08:41.340 --> 01:08:50.520
But for Fannie’s neighbors, they could&nbsp;
see $1 become $500 overnight. The FBI saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:55.020
an opportunity to rip it away from&nbsp;
them and give it to… someone else.

01:08:55.020 --> 01:09:01.140
Law enforcement called the Numbers game&nbsp;
racketeering, and in 1970 the FBI led&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.140 --> 01:09:06.900
what J Edgar Hoover called the largest&nbsp;
gambling raid in history, shutting down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.900 --> 01:09:14.040
58 Numbers operators. Then governments&nbsp;
realized that if the house always wins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:21.000
they could just… become the house. States made&nbsp;
Numbers illegal, did their best to wipe it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:21.000 --> 01:09:28.620
and then gave themselves a legal Numbers&nbsp;
monopoly. Wow! What a neat trick!

01:09:28.620 --> 01:09:34.740
Lotteries have actually had a really complex&nbsp;
history in the United States -- from the colonial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.740 --> 01:09:40.620
period to early statehood, hundreds of lotteries&nbsp;
helped raise money for schools and public works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:40.620 --> 01:09:47.400
By the 1830’s, lotteries were denounced by social&nbsp;
reformers as morally bankrupt, predatory games&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:55.860
that generated “tainted revenue” -- and in 1895&nbsp;
lottery games were banned nationwide. From legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.860 --> 01:10:02.520
lotteries to policy houses, there was enough&nbsp;
corruption and game-fixing to consider what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:02.520 --> 01:10:10.380
best for the public interest. Now 44 states have&nbsp;
legalized lotteries, and the Powerball is played&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.380 --> 01:10:17.520
in all of them -- to help raise money for schools&nbsp;
and public works. And we’ve come full circle.

01:10:18.120 --> 01:10:21.000
But the reality is to run a game like Numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.000 --> 01:10:25.140
what you really need is the&nbsp;
trust of your community.

01:10:25.140 --> 01:10:30.840
Fannie Davis and other boutique Numbers&nbsp;
operators engaged in a sort of prescient&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.840 --> 01:10:36.360
scholarship decades before neuroscience&nbsp;
and psychology research confirmed what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.360 --> 01:10:41.640
harnessed intuitively -- the community&nbsp;
element of Numbers was everything.

01:10:41.640 --> 01:10:46.020
They seized the power of Dunbar’s&nbsp;
Number, building on the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.020 --> 01:10:51.420
cohesion in well-connected neighborhoods so&nbsp;
everyone could play a game together — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.420 --> 01:10:58.740
that’s completely lost when 100,000 strangers&nbsp;
play a lottery anonymously at a gas station.

01:10:58.740 --> 01:11:05.220
They knew that throwing in just one additional&nbsp;
number meant only hitting straight once every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:05.220 --> 01:11:11.280
30 years -- that’s probably once in a&nbsp;
lifetime. And that’s not often enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:11.280 --> 01:11:17.220
for most people to wanna keep playing, even&nbsp;
if it increases the prizes dramatically.

01:11:17.220 --> 01:11:21.900
They intuited the communal strength&nbsp;
of Rene Girard’s mimetic desire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:21.900 --> 01:11:28.500
and how much people love playing&nbsp;
games together -- and need to.

01:11:28.500 --> 01:11:35.280
And they actually knew how to make a small economy&nbsp;
work: Numbers created tens of thousands of jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:45.120
within communities trying to survive in the face&nbsp;
of discrimination, segregation and rampant racism.

01:11:45.120 --> 01:11:52.440
A 1903 Detroit Free Press article said Numbers&nbsp;
was “largely the gambling field of the ignorant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.440 --> 01:11:56.760
superstitious, and poor… the&nbsp;
main thing is to have the evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:04.020
eternally wiped out.” Lottery ban legislation&nbsp;
was a patronizing attempt to protect communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:04.020 --> 01:12:10.140
from themselves. Now we’ve got Japan’s&nbsp;
Takarakuji, South Korea’s Lotto 6/45,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:10.140 --> 01:12:17.040
the US Mega Millions and Russia’s Gosloto -- in&nbsp;
addition to US state games that operate exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.040 --> 01:12:25.320
like Numbers did decades ago. Pennsylvania even&nbsp;
named their 3-digit lottery “The Daily Number.”

01:12:25.320 --> 01:12:34.200
But those games can’t do what people like Fannie&nbsp;
Davis did in Detroit. There’s no talk at the diner&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:40.560
about who won that week or who hasn’t hit in&nbsp;
ages. There’s no knock on the door to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:40.560 --> 01:12:47.760
your lucky payout the day after your numbers&nbsp;
match. There’s no playing 357 because you live&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.760 --> 01:12:56.460
at 357 Park Drive and everyone knowing when that&nbsp;
number hit – YOU were the winner. That’s all gone.

01:12:56.460 --> 01:13:01.680
There’s enough intrigue about Fannie Davis’s&nbsp;
life and her Numbers game to fill a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:01.680 --> 01:13:08.040
docuseries -- and there were thousands like her.&nbsp;
Read Bridgett Davis’s book about her mom -- that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.040 --> 01:13:13.800
her story to tell, not mine. Check out Felicia&nbsp;
George’s dissertation about Numbers in Detroit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:13.800 --> 01:13:18.360
or Louise Meriwether’s novel “Daddy&nbsp;
was a Number Runner” -- you’ve heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:18.360 --> 01:13:23.220
a million stories about the good guys&nbsp;
and bad guys during Prohibition. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:23.220 --> 01:13:29.640
Numbers stories like Miss Fannie’s&nbsp;
have been almost entirely ignored.

01:13:29.640 --> 01:13:30.780
But they shouldn’t be.

01:13:30.780 --> 01:13:38.400
Because alongside the rackets were people who&nbsp;
understood the social fabric of their communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:38.400 --> 01:13:42.600
Making your own way in the world and&nbsp;
making everyone else a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.600 --> 01:13:46.620
happier? That’s not the American&nbsp;
Dream, that’s everyone’s dream.

01:13:46.620 --> 01:13:53.880
But the highly-regulated mass-production of fun&nbsp;
strips the complex social function from a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:53.880 --> 01:14:03.540
that’s the perfect blend of math and people.&nbsp;
It’s reduced to lonely, anonymous gambling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.080 --> 01:14:09.900
And when that happens, the only&nbsp;
thing left is just… numbers.

01:14:10.920 --> 01:14:13.140
And as always, thanks for watching.

01:14:17.460 --> 01:14:23.880
Vsauce! Kevin here. And I’m about to bust the&nbsp;
greatest female tycoon of the 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.880 --> 01:14:31.200
using FORENSIC MATH -- I just need skim milk, an&nbsp;
amputated leg, and a family of nerds to do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:37.200
Millions of dollars were at stake --&nbsp;
and it all came down to handwriting.

01:14:50.580 --> 01:14:54.600
I’ve told some stories about how&nbsp;
math has perverted the pursuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:59.880
of justice and destroyed lives --&nbsp;
but when one of America’s richest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.880 --> 01:15:05.700
women tried to steal even more&nbsp;
money, math detected her fraud.

01:15:05.700 --> 01:15:12.540
Hetty Green was bred to make money -- and&nbsp;
she was the best at it. At 6 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.540 --> 01:15:17.340
Hetty was reading financial newspapers&nbsp;
to her father, who owned a whaling firm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:17.340 --> 01:15:23.700
in Massachusetts. She tracked stock prices&nbsp;
with her grandfather, and by age 13 was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:23.700 --> 01:15:30.780
family bookkeeper. In the 1850’s, the family&nbsp;
had MILLIONS -- which in today’s money is TENS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:37.860
of millions. But Hetty needed more. And&nbsp;
when her aunt died, she saw dollar signs.

01:15:37.860 --> 01:15:45.060
First, you need to understand Hetty. Her&nbsp;
entire LIFE was all about money -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.060 --> 01:15:49.800
she racked up a fortune that would be&nbsp;
valued well into the BILLIONS today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.460 --> 01:15:56.520
She was known as the “Witch of Wall Street”&nbsp;
-- but she was famously miserly. She was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:56.520 --> 01:16:01.920
just as obsessed with making money as&nbsp;
she was with never spending ANY of it.

01:16:01.920 --> 01:16:03.180
Here are some examples.

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:11.040
She spent so long trying to find a free clinic&nbsp;
to treat her son’s leg injury that she waited&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.040 --> 01:16:18.000
too long and it had to be amputated. She refused&nbsp;
to use hot water because fuel to heat it felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.000 --> 01:16:25.380
a waste of money. She wore one black dress and a&nbsp;
single set of underwear that she only changed when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.380 --> 01:16:30.540
they wore out. When she bailed out the New York&nbsp;
City government by writing a check to cover its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.540 --> 01:16:37.380
budgetary shortfalls, she was living in boarding&nbsp;
houses -- because she wanted to. She said, “Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:37.380 --> 01:16:44.460
because I dress plainly and do not spend a fortune&nbsp;
on my gowns, they say I am cranky or insane.”

01:16:44.460 --> 01:16:52.500
Hetty Green wasn’t insane -- but she was a&nbsp;
criminal. And that’s where the math comes in.

01:16:52.500 --> 01:16:58.980
When her aunt Sylvia died in 1865, her&nbsp;
will stipulated that about $1 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:58.980 --> 01:17:04.800
would go to charities. That’s about $15&nbsp;
million today. The other $1 million would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:04.800 --> 01:17:09.720
be put in a trust for Hetty. So&nbsp;
half for charity. Half for Hetty.

01:17:09.720 --> 01:17:11.280
Here’s what happened next...

01:17:11.280 --> 01:17:19.020
Hetty remarkably produced an earlier copy&nbsp;
of the will stating that 100% of her aunt’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:19.020 --> 01:17:25.680
fortune would go directly to her… which&nbsp;
seemed pretty convenient. It also had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:25.680 --> 01:17:31.620
page attached that invalidated any future&nbsp;
wills, which seemed… really convenient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.620 --> 01:17:36.900
The estate’s executor, Thomas Mandell,&nbsp;
saw it and was like, “I don’t think so.”

01:17:36.900 --> 01:17:43.140
So what did Hetty do? She sued the estate&nbsp;
-- and that’s when nerds came to the rescue.

01:17:43.140 --> 01:17:50.520
Charles Sanders Peirce was a literal genius&nbsp;
-- by age 23, he had 3 degrees from Harvard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.520 --> 01:17:57.780
He eventually became one of America’s greatest&nbsp;
logicians, including realizing in 1886 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:57.780 --> 01:18:04.620
electrical circuits could carry out logical&nbsp;
operations. That resulted in the digital computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:04.620 --> 01:18:13.560
decades later, but in 1865, he entered a courtroom&nbsp;
in Boston to analyze Aunt Sylvia’s handwriting.

01:18:13.560 --> 01:18:20.100
Charles compared 42 samples of Sylvia’s&nbsp;
signature and concluded that it had 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:20.100 --> 01:18:26.220
downstrokes -- in graphonomics, a “ballistic&nbsp;
stroke” that conveys a distinct change in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.220 --> 01:18:33.420
absolute velocity of the pen. My signature&nbsp;
has 18 -- 8 in Kevin, 10 in Lieber.

01:18:33.420 --> 01:18:39.540
Here’s a question. How consistent is a&nbsp;
signature? How much did Sylvia’s vary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:39.540 --> 01:18:43.080
from sample to sample? How much does MINE vary?

01:18:43.080 --> 01:18:50.460
I signed my name, and then I made 42 more&nbsp;
signatures -- and tried to make them match. Then I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:50.460 --> 01:18:57.240
judged each sample against the original signature&nbsp;
to find out how many of the 18 downstrokes in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:57.240 --> 01:19:03.180
name were the same -- I looked at size, length,&nbsp;
and angle to determine whether each stroke was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:03.180 --> 01:19:08.820
substantially similar. They followed a normal&nbsp;
distribution, and the result was an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:08.820 --> 01:19:17.940
of 7.405 matches out of a possible 18 -- so&nbsp;
only 41% the same. And I was really trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:17.940 --> 01:19:22.980
make them the same! Sylvia’s 42 signatures&nbsp;
were probably done over a period of years.

01:19:22.980 --> 01:19:30.360
But the interesting thing is the variance -- my&nbsp;
signatures have a standard deviation of 1.326,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.360 --> 01:19:36.120
which means that 99.73% of my&nbsp;
signatures should fall between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.120 --> 01:19:45.240
3.427 and 11.383 -- that’s a range of plus&nbsp;
and minus 3 sigma. The Z-score of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.240 --> 01:19:50.400
sample indicates how many standard deviations&nbsp;
that sample is from the mean -- basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:50.400 --> 01:19:59.460
how much of an outlier it is. My most deviant&nbsp;
sample was 10 matches, for a Z-score of 1.957.

01:19:59.460 --> 01:20:03.360
I actually did one more set of&nbsp;
comparisons just to make sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:10.260
I compared signature #21 to all the&nbsp;
others. The mean was 6.810 matches and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:10.260 --> 01:20:17.460
the standard deviation was 1.418. The&nbsp;
highest Z score was still only 2.249.

01:20:17.460 --> 01:20:24.900
Based on my 42 samples, how deviant would&nbsp;
a perfect 18-for-18 match actually be? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:24.900 --> 01:20:33.420
would have a Z-score of 7.989 -- nearly 8&nbsp;
standard deviations from the mean. The odds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:33.420 --> 01:20:38.040
of a signature being seven standard&nbsp;
deviations from the mean are about 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:38.040 --> 01:20:46.020
in 390 billion -- the odds of 7.989? Uhhh.&nbsp;
Figure it out and tell me in the comments.

01:20:46.020 --> 01:20:47.280
Back to Hetty.

01:20:47.280 --> 01:20:52.680
Charles Peirce found that Sylvia’s 42&nbsp;
samples had an average of 6 downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.680 --> 01:20:58.020
strokes matching the will’s signature&nbsp;
-- so about 20% -- and conformed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:58.020 --> 01:21:02.700
a binomial distribution just&nbsp;
like mine. And probably YOURS.

01:21:02.700 --> 01:21:07.860
When Charles compared the signature on the&nbsp;
first page of the will to the signature on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:07.860 --> 01:21:15.060
the attached second page, he found that&nbsp;
all 30 downstrokes were a perfect match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.060 --> 01:21:23.160
A 100% perfect match. His father Benjamin&nbsp;
Peirce was a Harvard mathematician -- he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.160 --> 01:21:29.820
testified that the probability of Sylvia’s&nbsp;
second signature being a perfect 30 for 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:29.820 --> 01:21:44.640
match was 2.666 x 10^21 -- or 1 in 2&nbsp;
sextillion, 666 quintillion. So yeah.

01:21:44.640 --> 01:21:51.300
Benjamin Peirce said, “Such evanescent&nbsp;
shadows of probability cannot belong to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:51.300 --> 01:21:59.220
actual life.” Which is an amazing quote. Uhhh&nbsp;
yeah, Hetty traced Aunt Sylvia’s signature.

01:21:59.220 --> 01:22:04.680
She lost the case and fled with her husband&nbsp;
to London when the family threatened to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:04.680 --> 01:22:10.920
her indicted for fraud. But… she didn’t&nbsp;
really lose. After 5 years in court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:10.920 --> 01:22:18.480
she was awarded $600,000 from the estate -- less&nbsp;
than the original legitimate will gave her, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.480 --> 01:22:26.580
still $12 million in today’s money. And the state&nbsp;
never brought criminal charges against... the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.580 --> 01:22:34.440
wealthiest woman living in it. The following year,&nbsp;
Hetty had a daughter -- who she named Sylvia.

01:22:34.440 --> 01:22:42.480
Hetty Green died during a heated argument with her&nbsp;
maid about the benefits of skim milk. Today her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.480 --> 01:22:50.220
estate would be valued at $5 billion. She really&nbsp;
was America’s first female business tycoon -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:50.220 --> 01:22:56.400
she wasn’t all bad. She became an anonymous&nbsp;
philanthropist and served as a nurse to care for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:56.400 --> 01:23:06.960
friends and family. In 1951, Sylvia Green died,&nbsp;
leaving over 99% of the family fortune to charity.

01:23:06.960 --> 01:23:13.620
I’ve made videos about the crazy odds of two&nbsp;
people having the same name -- and having weirdly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.620 --> 01:23:20.940
similar fingerprints -- and having rare tragedies&nbsp;
strike twice in the same family. I’ve shown that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:20.940 --> 01:23:28.800
math alone is a terrible basis for convicting&nbsp;
someone of a crime -- and insane odds can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:28.800 --> 01:23:35.880
mean innocence. With Sylvia Ann Howland’s&nbsp;
will, we have to consider the totality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.880 --> 01:23:43.020
the circumstances: Hetty’s motive, her obsessive&nbsp;
greed, her miraculous production of a will that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:43.020 --> 01:23:52.020
just happened to be entirely in her favor -- all&nbsp;
combined with truly astronomical signature odds.

01:23:52.020 --> 01:23:57.300
Knowing when not to believe in&nbsp;
coincidences is just as crucial as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.300 --> 01:24:02.760
accepting them. 1 in a billion? Okay --&nbsp;
if 1 in a billion people won a lottery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:02.760 --> 01:24:08.520
that means about 8 people alive right&nbsp;
now would hit the jackpot. It’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:08.520 --> 01:24:17.040
impossible. A mountain of evidence plus 1 in&nbsp;
2.6 sextillion? Yeah. I don’t think so, Hetty.

01:24:17.040 --> 01:24:21.360
Hetty Green spent a lifetime accumulating money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:21.360 --> 01:24:26.940
power, and influence -- and she could never&nbsp;
get enough of any of it. But in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:26.940 --> 01:24:34.260
all of Hetty’s precious millions couldn’t&nbsp;
compete with the Peirce family’s sextillions.

01:24:34.260 --> 01:24:37.080
And as always, thanks for watching.

01:24:38.820 --> 01:24:44.400
So there’s an important point about&nbsp;
why I chose these stories. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:44.400 --> 01:24:49.380
statistical outliers. They’re the&nbsp;
extremes. Most cases don’t need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.380 --> 01:24:55.080
multi-year analysis of expected health&nbsp;
outcomes or poring over 30 downstrokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:55.080 --> 01:25:01.620
of 42 different signatures. These stories&nbsp;
are interesting because they’re unusual.

01:25:01.620 --> 01:25:06.720
But anomalies and aberrations often&nbsp;
lay bare the problems we have with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:06.720 --> 01:25:12.540
the mundane. Brandon Mayfield’s case&nbsp;
happened after 100 years of forensic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:12.540 --> 01:25:17.640
fingerprint analysis -- that’s a&nbsp;
full century of misunderstandings.

01:25:17.640 --> 01:25:21.960
The truth is that highlighting the&nbsp;
extremes is often the most compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:21.960 --> 01:25:27.540
and illuminating way to understand&nbsp;
how generally fragile our grip is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:27.540 --> 01:25:31.860
concepts like basic probability&nbsp;
or identifying your attacker.

01:25:31.860 --> 01:25:38.580
We’ve spent thousands of years trying&nbsp;
to understand and refine justice --&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:38.580 --> 01:25:39.900
and we’re just getting started.

01:25:40.800 --> 01:25:43.080
And as always, thanks for watching.

